"""Class 10 SST Ch 13 Power-sharing - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"The Belgian model of power-sharing has helped the country remain united.", r"In Belgium the Constitution requires equal numbers of Dutch and French-speaking ministers in the central government and gives community governments powers over cultural, educational and language matters.", "a",
     r"Accommodating both communities and not letting the majority dominate prevented conflict; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"In Sri Lanka the Sinhala-speaking community forms the majority of the population.", r"The Sinhala-speaking community of Sri Lanka lives mainly in the north and east of the island.", "c",
     r"A is true (about 74 per cent). R is false: the Sri Lankan Tamils live mainly in the north and east."),
 AR4(r"Power-sharing is considered good because it reduces the possibility of conflict between social groups.", r"Power-sharing is a moral reason as it is the very spirit of democracy.", "b",
     r"The first is a prudential reason and the second a moral reason; both are true but R does not explain A."),
 AR4(r"The majoritarian approach of Sri Lanka brought national unity.", r"The 1956 Act recognised Sinhala as the only official language.", "d",
     r"A is false: majoritarianism alienated the Tamils and led to civil conflict. R is true."),
 AR4(r"In federal governments, power is divided between the central government and state governments.", r"This is called vertical division of power because different levels of government exercise power.", "a",
     r"Sharing power between higher and lower levels of government is known as vertical division; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Brussels, the capital of Belgium, has a special arrangement of government.", r"In Belgium the majority of the population is Dutch-speaking and lives in the Flemish region.", "b",
     r"Both are true: in Brussels the Dutch-speaking community has equal representation in spite of being a minority, but R does not explain this arrangement as a whole."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Belgium has a small population of about 1 crore. Of its people, 59 per cent live in the Flemish region and speak Dutch, 40 per cent live in the Wallonia region and speak French, and the rest speak German. In the capital Brussels, 80 per cent speak French and 20 per cent speak Dutch. The minority French-speaking community was relatively rich and powerful. "
   r"(i) Which language is spoken by the majority in Belgium? (ii) Which language is spoken by most people in Brussels, and how does this differ from the country as a whole? (iii) What problem arose as the Dutch-speaking people became better off? (iv) How did Belgium solve its problem?",
   r"(i) Dutch (1). (ii) French, although French speakers are a minority in the whole country (1). (iii) The Dutch speakers demanded their due share, creating tension between the two communities (1). (iv) By amending the Constitution between 1970 and 1993, equal numbers of Dutch and French-speaking ministers in the central government, state governments with powers and community governments elected by one language group (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Sri Lanka became independent in 1948. Sinhala speakers form about 74 per cent of the people and Tamil speakers about 18 per cent. Leaders of the Sinhala community wanted to secure dominance over government. In 1956 an Act was passed to recognise only Sinhala as the official language, disregarding Tamil. "
   r"(i) In which year did Sri Lanka become independent? (ii) What was the Act of 1956? (iii) What kind of approach did the Sinhala leaders follow? (iv) What was the result of this approach on the Tamil community?",
   r"(i) 1948 (1). (ii) An Act recognising Sinhala as the only official language (1). (iii) A majoritarian approach (1). (iv) The Tamils felt alienated, as they got fewer government jobs and university posts and no recognition of their language, so their demand for autonomy grew and led to a civil war (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In a district, a village panchayat, a block samiti and a district-level body each have separate powers. Meanwhile, at the national level, the legislature, executive and judiciary exercise different powers in the same government. Also, in some states, many parties join to form a coalition government. A student is asked to identify the forms of power-sharing. "
   r"(i) Name the form of power-sharing between legislature, executive and judiciary. (ii) What is power-sharing between the central, state and local governments called? (iii) Which form of power-sharing is seen in a coalition government? (iv) Why is a system of checks and balances useful?",
   r"(i) Horizontal division of power (1). (ii) Vertical division of power (1). (iii) Power-sharing among different political parties (1). (iv) No organ can exercise unlimited power, since each checks the others, so power is not misused and the rule of law is maintained (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

MISTAKES = [
 r"Mixing up the two reasons for power-sharing: <b>prudential</b> (reduces social conflict, keeps stability) and <b>moral</b> (power-sharing is the spirit of democracy).",
 r"Quoting Belgium's and Sri Lanka's data interchangeably: Belgium <b>59% Dutch, 40% French</b>; Sri Lanka <b>74% Sinhala, 18% Tamil</b>.",
 r"Calling the 1956 Sri Lankan Act a step toward equality: it made <b>Sinhala the only official language</b> and favoured the majority.",
 r"Confusing horizontal and vertical division of power: <b>horizontal</b> = among organs (legislature, executive, judiciary); <b>vertical</b> = among levels of government (Union, state, local).",
 r"Forgetting that Belgium is <b>not</b> majoritarian: it gave equal ministers to Dutch and French speakers even though Dutch speakers are the majority.",
 r"Writing 'community government' for the Belgian state government; the community government is elected by people of <b>one language community</b> irrespective of where they live.",
]
